CISC 3130 - Music Player Write-Up

Part A: Big-O Table
*n = number of songs, q = queue entries checked, and h = history entries checked*

    Method       | Structures it touches                  | Big-O    | One-line reason
-----------------|----------------------------------------|----------|-----------------------------------------------
    addSong      | HashMap, Doubly Linked List            | O(1)     | Adds a song at the end and saves it in HashMap.
    findSong     | HashMap                                | O(1)     | Finds a song using its ID.
    play         | HashMap, Stack                         | O(1)     | Finds and plays a song and updates its count.
    queueLater   | HashMap, Deque                         | O(1)     | Adds a song to the back of the queue.
    queueNext    | HashMap, Deque                         | O(1)     | Adds a song to the front of the queue.
    next         | Deque, HashMap, Linked List, Stack     | O(q + 1) | Skips removed songs and plays the next song.
    back         | Stack, HashMap, Deque                  | O(h + 1) | Skips removed songs and plays the previous one.
    removeSong   | HashMap, Doubly Linked List            | O(1)     | Removes a song by updating its links.
    showPlaylist | Doubly Linked List, ArrayList          | O(n)     | Goes through all songs to show the playlist.


Part B: Defend Two Choices

1. Doubly Linked List — Playlist
I used a doubly linked list so that it can  store the songs in my playlist. It allowed me to move between songs using
the next and previous links. If I had used a regular array, removing a song could require shifting other songs, which
takes O(n) time. But with my doubly linked list and HashMap, I can remove a song in O(1) average time. Building my own
linked list takes more code than using Java's built-in LinkedList, but it gives me more control over how the songs are
connected.

2. Deque — Up Next Queue
I used an ArrayDeque to manage the songs that will play next. It basically allowed me to add songs to the front or back
of the queue in O(1) time. If I had used a regular array, adding a song to the front of the queue could require
shifting other songs, which takes O(n) time. And java's built-in ArrayDeque makes my code easier because I don't have
to manage the array size or move the elements of the queue myself. It cost me to have less control over its internal
implementation.

